WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.480
OpenAI just dropped colorful blob-shaped ai agents that build virtual worlds. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 184. At its yearly builder event called DevDay, OpenAI showed a product named Dots, the Verge Reports. An agent is software that takes a goal and works through several steps without waiting for a new nudge every time. Treat it like a movie sidekick who stays on the assignment, not a snack machine that drops one answer and goes dark.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:35.200
A regular chat bot takes one question, replies, then sits still until you poke it again.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:40.720
Dots can sketch a scene, then edit characters, then shift the lighting when you say so.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.180
Those moves chain together inside the same chat, instead of dying after a single reply.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.660
It also keeps what you already asked, the way a saved game holds your spot.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.760
You can walk away, come back, and the work is still in progress.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:01.020
For class, a rough idea can become an interactive story or a tiny game prototype in one sitting.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:05.460
You are not stuck re-explaining the plot across a pile of separate messages.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.720
Anyone curious about future work gets a look at teammate style roles in design, game development

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.040
and content creation.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.480
Access is limited to paid chat GPT Pro, business, or enterprise plans.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:23.580
If school or family already got you in, open a new chat and give it a job with more than

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:29.460
one beat. Try typing, create a simple forest scene, and then add a moving character, then

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:35.120
watch it keep going. No paid plan yet? Free Dev Day recap videos on YouTube show the

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.200
same flow from start to finish. Longer jobs need a different kind of memory than a one

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.240
and done reply. Think about cutting a group TikTok, where one person holds the shot list

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.820
while others handle clips, text, and music. The lead agent keeps a running list of everything

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.520
you asked for, like that shot list on a phone note.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.040
It slices the big request into smaller chores and ticks them off in order.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.400
It can pass pieces to helper models with different skills, one that draws pictures and one

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.120
that writes words.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.820
When those helpers finish, the lead agent stitches the parts into one result you can actually see.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.920
It is not sitting there having feelings about your forest.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.900
It is following patterns learned from huge numbers of examples of people solving problems

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:18.500
in order.

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.680
The closest everyday picture is a shared checklist that never gets bored and never checks

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.580
a second screen.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:30.120
After that, a long creative job stops sounding like magic and starts sounding like teamwork

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.540
with a stubborn organizer.

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:37.760
Google put a feature called Guided Vision inside the Gemini Live app on Android phones, the

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:38.360
Verge Reports.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.360
Gemini Live is Google's live assistant that can look through your camera while you talk.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.140
point the lens at tiny print, a medicine label, a foreign menu, or a dense

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.620
instruction sheet. The assistant describes it out loud in real time like a

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.880
classmate reading the fine print you cannot squint through. That helps when you

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.120
are in a hurry or the type is painfully small. Open Gemini Live, tap the camera

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:07.440
icon and aim at the text. Then say, read this out loud and tell me what it means in

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.580
simple words, and listen for a plain language version. If the app asks for an

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.660
account, a parent can help with signup.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.920
People who care about class presentations are also using chat tools to escape boring

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:20.920
default templates.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.300
The assistant can propose layouts, visual jokes, and clear diagrams that match what you

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.480
actually want to say.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.400
Slides start to feel closer to a poster a stylish classmate helped you rebuild, not a wall

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:33.940
of bullets.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.200
For a project do soon, open any chat tool you already use and paste your notes.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.600
Type, turn these bullet points into a fun 5-slide presentation with simple diagrams.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:50.040
Copy the best ideas into PowerPoint or Google Slides and tweak the wording so it sounds

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:50.520
like you.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.500
Same time as the old grind, much less cardboard on the screen.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:04:01.220
One creator built a full car commercial with simple 3D shapes and AI video tools, for almost

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:02.780
nothing next to a real shoot.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:08.900
Low poly means blocky models with few sides, closer to toy bricks than a glossy magazine

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:14.420
photo. The trick is mixing a few high-quality bits with lots of cheap, low-detail ones,

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.480
so the cut still looks polished. A new model called Griffin is being described as the

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.620
first to pass a video touring test. That test asks whether people can tell a machine

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.760
from a real human in conversation. It fooled almost half the viewers into thinking they

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.080
were talking to a person. The chats felt natural and moved both ways without those awkward

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:41.520
frozen gaps. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh? That's cool. Go play

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.920
with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. If this show earns a place in

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.460
your day, follow it in your podcast app so the next episode is waiting for you. And if you'd rather

00:04:53.460 --> 00:05:05.060
watch, then listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network. Links in the show notes. One more thing. If

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.520
is worth a spot in your feed.

